.TH MU2EDAQ-BIGREDBOX 1 "2026-07-22" "mu2edaq-bigredbox 2.0.0" "Mu2e DAQ Manual"
.SH NAME
mu2edaq\-bigredbox \- Mu2e DAQ critical alert listener ("Big Red Box")
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2edaq\-bigredbox
.br
.B python \-m mu2edaq_bigredbox
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mu2edaq\-bigredbox
listens for JSON critical\-error alerts broadcast over UDP by Mu2e DAQ
systems and displays a prominent always\-on\-top PyQt6 alert window for
each one.  It is normally started as a background daemon by the control
room via
.BR crs-app (1)
using the
.B start\-mu2edaq\-bigredbox.sh
wrapper script.
.PP
A GUI environment is required; on Linux the
.B DISPLAY
environment variable must be set.
.PP
If the optional
.B mu2edaq\-discovery
package is installed, the listener advertises itself as service
.RB \(dq bigredbox \(dq
so it appears in
.BR mu2edaq\-discover (1)
scans.  Its absence is not an error.
.SH DISCOVERY RESPONSE
In reply to a DISCOVER request the listener announces its package version in
the
.B version
field and the runtime detail below under
.BR meta ,
so an operator can confirm what is deployed on a node without logging into it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
{"app": "bigredbox", "name": "Big Red Box Alerts",
 "port": 37020, "scheme": "udp", "version": "2.0.0",
 "meta": {"version": "2.0.0", "qt": "6.11.0", "pyqt": "6.11.0",
          "python": "3.12.1", "udp_port": "37020"}}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Query it with:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
mu2edaq\-discover \-\-filter app=bigredbox \-\-json
.fi
.RE
.SH PAYLOAD FORMAT
Alerts are UTF\-8 JSON datagrams:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
{"system_id": "DAQ\-NODE\-01",
 "timestamp": "2026\-07\-22T09:15:00",
 "message": "Readout buffer overflow"}
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CRS_PORT_UDP
UDP port to listen on.  Exported by the control room from
.IR apps.yaml .
Takes precedence over the built\-in default of 37020.
.TP
.B DISPLAY
X11 display to open alert windows on.  Required on Linux.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/daq_alert.pid
PID file written at startup and removed on clean shutdown.
.TP
.I /tmp/daq_alert.log
Application log.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop the UDP listener, remove the PID file and exit cleanly.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown (the Qt event loop's exit code).
.TP
.B 3
The UDP port is already in use, meaning another listener is running.  The
program refuses to start a second instance and exits immediately.
.SH PAUSING ALERTS
Each alert window carries a
.B Pause
checkbox.  While it is ticked the application stops processing incoming alerts
altogether: no new windows open and no open window is updated.  Suppressed
alerts are dropped, not queued, and each one is recorded in the log.
.PP
Pause is global rather than per\-window \(em ticking it on one window
suppresses alerts for all of them.  Processing resumes as soon as no open
window has it ticked, either by unticking it or by closing that window.
.SH SINGLE INSTANCE
Only one listener may own the UDP port at a time.  The port is bound before
the PID file is written, so the binding \(em not the PID file \(em is what
decides which process is the live listener.  A second invocation exits with
status 3 rather than running on with a dead listener thread.
.PP
.B stop\-mu2edaq\-bigredbox.sh
falls back to the process holding the UDP port when the PID file is missing,
so a listener started by hand can still be stopped.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run in the foreground:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
mu2edaq\-bigredbox
.fi
.RE
.PP
Listen on a non\-default port:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
CRS_PORT_UDP=37030 mu2edaq\-bigredbox
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2edaq\-bigredbox\-send (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Mu2e DAQ Group, Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory.
